;;; **Refused at load**, and kept as that refusal's fixture: an obligation on
;;; a closed relation (Q-M1f.244). `(total-owed r is-a)` owes `r`, and
;;; `infer-closure` derives `(__closed__ r)` from `(functional r)` and
;;; `(total r)`, so the program says both that a state owes `r` a fact and that
;;; no commitment extends `r`: *"obligated relation can't be __closed__
;;; also"*. Every command refuses it, one line naming the obligation, its
;;; activator, the closure and the rule that derives it, exit 1 (`ein test`:
;;; 2). Its twin `tests/stdlib/closure/02_closed_and_satisfied.ein` keeps the
;;; closure and drops the obligation, and loads.
;;;
;;; `std.closure/infer-closure` — closed and **owing**: a state that can never
;;; meet its requirement. The program is `02_closed_and_satisfied.ein` with
;;; one fact deleted, the witness edge `(r a1 b1)`, and the obligation added.
;;;
;;; ── Why nothing else catches it ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `a1` is an `A`, `r` is declared total, and `r` has no edges. So the
;;; requirement *every A has some B* is unmet. It is also **unreachable**:
;;;
;;;   - no rule derives an `r`-fact — there is no rule here but the observer;
;;;   - no hypothesis can propose one, because `functional ∧ total` derived
;;;     `(__closed__ r)` and closure is exactly the instruction never to
;;;     speculate about `r`.
;;;
;;; And `std.algebra/total` — the scan whose job is to say *the requirement
;;; has become unreachable* — cannot fire, because it is written
;;; open-world-safe: it demands a **stored negative** for every candidate
;;; (`(forall ?b (?isa ?b ?B) (not (?R ?a ?b)))`) before it concludes
;;; anything, and in a program with no negative-completion rule no such
;;; negative will ever be stored. The scan is activated, correct, and silent.
;;;
;;; Without the obligation, nothing checks the requirement: the generator,
;;; closed, proposes nothing, and the state is complete by exhaustion. With
;;; it, the state owes a debt no commitment can pay, and that pair is what
;;; the loader refuses.
;;;
;;; ── The three ingredients, and each is individually reasonable ─────────────
;;;
;;; Worth stating because no one of them is the bug:
;;;
;;;   1. `infer-closure`'s operational witness is opt-in and boxed-warned
;;;      (`01_infer_closure.ein` exhibits its completeness hazard);
;;;   2. `total`'s stored-negative discipline is what stops it firing `(false)`
;;;      on every empty-yet state, and is the *right* way to write it —
;;;      `connex` is what the other way looks like;
;;;   3. `total-owed`, the obligation dual, says what a state owes `r`, and an
;;;      obligation needs no closed domain (Q-M1d.3).
;;;
;;; The corner is their intersection. `(false)` is a **derived** refutation —
;;; a rule fired and named a conflict — and no rule fires here; promoting on
;;; "the debt is unpayable" would be a closed-world inference the engine
;;; refuses everywhere else. So the program is refused at load instead, where
;;; the conflict is in what it writes, not in any state.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation observes ──────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Nothing: the program is refused before any claim is read. The `:expect`
;;; below states the owing state such a program would have, its derived
;;; closure made nameable by `closed-here`; all three `:expect` forms are
;;; assertions about *facts*, and an `open` conclusion is never one.
;;;
;;; ── What `ein solve` prints ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   kb load error: examples/ein-bugs/closed-and-owing.ein:124:2: obligation
;;;   'total-owed', activated by (total-owed r is-a), written at …:124:2,
;;;   owes r, and (__closed__ r), which rule 'infer-closure' derives, says r
;;;   is closed — an obligated relation is not closed: an obligation is paid
;;;   by facts of the relation it owes, so a state that owes r and may not be
;;;   given one is stuck; drop the closure, or the obligation
;;;
;;; `ein saturate` and `ein oracle` print the same line and exit 1, and
;;; `ein test` exits 2. Line 124 is the `(total-owed r is-a)` below; every
;;; line of this file keeps its place, since the refusal's position is part
;;; of what the fixture records.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;

(import std.algebra :symbols (functional total total-owed))
(import std.closure :symbols (infer-closure))

(relation is-a        T T :why "{?1} is a {?2}")
(relation r           A B :why "{?1} is assigned {?2}")
(relation closed-here T   :why "{?1}'s extension was closed by infer-closure")

(is-a a1 A)
(is-a b1 B) (is-a b2 B)

;; The markers `infer-closure` reads (arity 1) …
(functional r)
(total      r)
;; … and the activator the unreachability scan needs (arity 2). It is
;; activated here and it never fires — see above.
(total r is-a)
;; … and the obligation dual, which derives nothing either — it *owes*. This
;; single line, beside the derived closure, is what the loader refuses
;; (Q-M1f.244).
(total-owed r is-a)

(rule closed-observed ()
  :match  (__closed__ ?R)
  :assert (closed-here ?R)
  :why    "{?R}'s extension is closed")

;; No `(r a1 b1)`. `a1` owes a `B` it can never be given.

(solve
  ;; `r` is closed by derivation; the other two are scoped out by hand so the
  ;; blind generator is quiet and the verdict is about the requirement alone.
  :no-hypothesis (is-a closed-here)
  :expect (region (owing (model (closed-here r)))))

(query
  :goal (closed-here ?R))
